---
/**
 * The opening screen.
 *
 * A blank field with the name writing itself, which then lifts to reveal the
 * page. Shown once per visit, on the homepage.
 *
 * A screen that covers the site is the riskiest thing on it, so three
 * properties matter more than the animation does:
 *
 * 1. **It cannot be stranded.** It is dismissed by a CSS animation that ends
 *    hidden, not by JavaScript. Script only ever starts it. If script dies
 *    immediately afterwards, the screen still lifts on time.
 * 2. **It never waits for anything.** No load event, no asset, no network. A
 *    fixed length, decided up front. The usual failure here is a screen that
 *    waits for something that never arrives and leaves the site blank while
 *    every request returned perfectly normally.
 * 3. **It cannot block the page.** It ignores the pointer throughout, so the
 *    content underneath stays clickable even while it is on screen.
 *
 * It is absent entirely for anyone who has asked for less motion, anyone who
 * has already seen it this visit, and anyone without JavaScript — the markup
 * stays inert until a script in the document head opts into it.
 */
/*
 * The signature, and only here. The hero and the footer keep the lettering, so
 * this screen no longer reads from the same file as they do — swapping the one
 * used here used to swap all three.
 */
import signature from "@/assets/logo/signature.svg?raw"
---

<div class="intro" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="intro__mark">
    <span class="intro__ghost" set:html={signature} />
    <span class="intro__ink" set:html={signature} />
  </div>
</div>

<style is:global>
  /*
   * Global for the same reason as the lettering's own styles: the markup below
   * is injected, so it never receives Astro's scoping attribute and a scoped
   * rule would match nothing. Namespaced under `.intro` instead.
   *
   * The timing, in one place:
   *
   *   writing   60ms wait, then one sweep of 640ms              → 700ms
   *   hold      150ms                                           → 850ms
   *   slide     450ms                                           → 1300ms
   *
   * The hero lettering starts rising at 850ms, so the reveal and the arrival
   * overlap instead of queueing.
   *
   * One sweep rather than a letter at a time. The lettering could be cut into
   * ten pieces because it is ten separate letters; a signature is one
   * unbroken stroke, and cutting it into columns would show slices arriving
   * rather than a hand moving. Left to right in one pass is also simply what
   * writing looks like.
   *
   * The length is set by Speed Index, not by taste. Speed Index measures how
   * soon the page looks finished, and mobile Lighthouse scores it at about
   * 1.4 times what the filmstrip shows, so a screen that holds the page white
   * for two seconds reads as a page that took nearly five to load. At 2.8
   * seconds, with the hero arriving until 3.5, this screen alone held the
   * homepage at 97 on mobile PageSpeed while every load metric scored full
   * marks. Scoring 100 needs the page to look finished within about 1.5
   * seconds. Everything that makes it safe — it never waits on anything, it
   * cannot be stranded, it never takes the pointer — holds at any length.
   */
  .intro {
    display: none;
  }

  /*
   * Everything here is gated on `data-intro`, which the script in the document
   * head only sets when the reader has not asked for less motion — so the
   * reduced-motion path is "none of this exists" rather than a media query in
   * this file. See the opening-screen script in BaseLayout.
   */
  :root[data-intro] .intro {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 100;
    background-color: var(--color-paper);
    /* Never intercepts a click, even mid-fade. */
    pointer-events: none;
    animation: intro-lift 1300ms linear forwards;
  }

  /*
   * A fifth of the width, with a floor and two ceilings.
   *
   * The floor is what keeps it a greeting on a phone. A share of the width
   * alone reads well on a desktop and collapses on a small screen: at 375px it
   * came out 75px across, which is narrower than the same mark in the bar —
   * an opening screen showing the name smaller than the page behind it does.
   *
   * The height matters as well as the width because this mark is 1.73:1 where
   * the lettering was 5.3:1, so the same width makes it three times as tall.
   * `95vh` as a width holds it to roughly half the window's height, for a
   * window that is short and wide.
   *
   * The last term is the only one that is a taste decision rather than a
   * guard: past that size it stops reading as a signature and starts reading
   * as a banner.
   */
  :root[data-intro] .intro__mark {
    width: clamp(180px, min(20vw, 95vh), 760px);
    position: relative;
  }

  .intro__mark span {
    display: block;
  }

  /*
   * The pale copy is structural rather than decoration: it holds the finished
   * shape from the first frame, so the ink arrives into a line that is already
   * there instead of appearing out of nothing.
   */
  .intro__ghost {
    color: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 12%, transparent);
  }

  .intro__ink {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  .intro__mark svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    /*
     * The box is drawn tight to the ink, so rounding can shave the outermost
     * row of pixels off an edge that lands exactly on it.
     */
    overflow: visible;
  }

  /*
   * What moves is the edge the ink is clipped to, never the drawing. Stroking
   * an outline and animating the dash offset traces its perimeter — up one
   * side and back down the other — which on a brush stroke draws two thin
   * lines converging rather than a stroke being laid down. Uncovering the
   * finished drawing avoids that, and the last frame is identical to the
   * drawing standing still.
   */
  :root[data-intro] .intro__ink {
    clip-path: inset(0 100% 0 0);
    animation-name: mark-write;
    animation-duration: 640ms;
    animation-timing-function: var(--ease-draw);
    animation-fill-mode: forwards;
    animation-delay: 60ms;
  }

  /*
   * Both ends of the sweep are written out, in the same unit. Leaving the
   * start to be read off the element works, but it means the animation only
   * makes sense read together with the rule above it, and a later edit to that
   * rule changes where this begins without touching these lines.
   */
  @keyframes mark-write {
    from {
      clip-path: inset(0 100% 0 0);
    }

    to {
      clip-path: inset(0 0% 0 0);
    }
  }

  /*
   * Holds, then slides up out of the way. Ending hidden is what makes this
   * self-dismissing.
   *
   * It slides rather than fades. Fading crossfades two different compositions
   * — the centred word on this screen and the large cropped one in the hero —
   * and for a few hundred milliseconds you see both at once, at different
   * sizes, overlapping. Sliding reveals the page underneath cleanly instead,
   * with nothing doubled.
   */
  @keyframes intro-lift {
    0% {
      transform: translateY(0);
      visibility: visible;
      animation-timing-function: linear;
    }
    /* 850ms of 1300: the end of the hold. */
    65.4% {
      transform: translateY(0);
      animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1);
    }
    100% {
      transform: translateY(-100%);
      visibility: hidden;
    }
  }

  /* The hero lettering waits for the reveal, but only when there is one. */
  :root[data-intro] .intro-follows {
    --hero-enter-delay: 850ms;
  }
</style>
